Title: "Integers"
Id: "3919403c-8205-49f9-aebb-fc23b59961ae"


Text: "The natural numbers can be added but not freely subtracted: there is no
       natural number equal to $3 - 5$. The integers repair this by adjoining
       negative numbers. The classical construction, carried out here,
       represents each integer as a difference of two naturals -- the pair
       $(n, m)$ standing for $n - m$ -- and then identifies pairs that ought to
       name the same difference. This is exactly a quotient by an equivalence
       relation, so it draws on every earlier chapter at once: the naturals,
       their addition, the Cartesian product, and the quotient of a set by a
       relation."
Id: "6ab727ff-4d3c-467a-a090-23baa9056340"


SectionTitle: "The Integer Equivalence Relation"
Id: "c4c04ee9-fb37-4bb1-9bfc-fbb273c455f7"


Text: "Two pairs $(n_1, m_1)$ and $(n_2, m_2)$ represent the same integer when
       $n_1 - m_1 = n_2 - m_2$. Since subtraction is not yet available, this is
       phrased using addition alone, as $n_1 + m_2 = n_2 + m_1$. The relation so
       defined is an equivalence relation on pairs of naturals."
Id: "86f693bc-1d69-4bfd-bef4-c67033b73acb"


[\integers.relation]
Defines: R is \equivalence.relation:on{\naturals \.set.cross./ \naturals}
expresses:
. forAll: n1, m1, n2, m2 is \natural
  then:
  . have: R((n1, m1), (n2, m2))
    iff: (n1 \.natural.+./ m2) = (n2 \.natural.+./ m1)
Documented:
. called: "integer equivalence relation"
. description: "The equivalence relation on $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$
                identifying pairs $(n_1, m_1)$ and $(n_2, m_2)$ when
                $n_1 + m_2 = n_2 + m_1$."
Id: "fd0234b9-545e-4dcf-bc4f-8c1c6f306e25"


SectionTitle: "The Structure of the Integers"
Id: "b1e96b53-1a5b-49bf-a250-bb2fb3114191"


[\integers]
Defines: Zb ::= (Z, 0, 1, +, *, neg(z_), leq(a_, b_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. Z is \set
. 0 "in" Z
. 1 "in" Z
. + is \binary.operation:on{Z}
. * is \binary.operation:on{Z}
. neg is \function:on{Z}:to{Z}
. leq is \total.order:on{Z}
expresses:
. 0 != 1
. forAll: a, b, c "in" Z
  then:
  . (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)
  . a + b = b + a
  . a + 0 = a
  . a + neg(a) = 0
  . (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
  . a * b = b * a
  . a * 1 = a
  . 1 * a = a
  . a * (b + c) = (a * b) + (a * c)
  . if: leq(a, b)
    then: leq(a + c, b + c)
. forAll: a, b "in" Z
  where:
  . leq(0, a)
  . leq(0, b)
  then: leq(0, a * b)
Enables:
. capability: z_ "in" Zb :-> z_ is \integer
. view:
  as: S := Z is \set
Documented:
. called: "the integers"
. written: "\mathbb{Z}"
. description: "The integers $(\mathbb{Z}, 0, 1, +, \cdot, -)$ as an algebraic
                ring constructed from the differences of natural numbers."
Id: "fe3288e5-96fd-4839-bd2f-1bc13160214f"


[\integer]
Declares: z "in" \integers
Enables:
. capability: a_ + b_ :=> a_ \.integer.+./ b_
. capability: a_ * b_ :=> a_ \.integer.*./ b_
. capability: -a_ :=> \integer.neg(a_)
. capability: a_ <= b_ :=> a_ \:integer.leq:/ b_
Documented:
. called: "integer"
. description: "An integer is an element of the underlying set of integers
                $\mathbb{Z}$."
Id: "a97ad153-f94d-4eb6-b91e-84f49114ab6e"


SectionTitle: "Integer Constants and Operations"
Id: "c0a1f8a4-c48a-4a84-8f81-0b741cd08c77"


Text: "The zero integer is the additive identity, and one is the multiplicative
       identity."
Id: "c29a27c7-6869-4b83-8183-70312240df75"


[\integer.zero]
Defines: z := \integers..0 is \integer
Documented:
. called: "integer zero"
. written: "0"
. description: "The integer zero $0 \in \mathbb{Z}$."
Id: "14e8b6fe-08ca-49d3-9cf9-152d052cb10d"


[\integer.one]
Defines: o := \integers..1 is \integer
expresses: o != \integer.zero
Documented:
. called: "integer one"
. written: "1"
. description: "The integer one $1 \in \mathbb{Z}$."
Id: "5cda05b0-3930-47cc-b00c-386456f52ab0"


Text: "Addition of integers is commutative and associative, with zero as
       identity."
Id: "66a4fe57-0841-4c98-afd8-1ab256038d71"


[a_ \.integer.+./ b_]
Defines: a_ + b_ is \binary.operation:on{\integers}
expresses: a_ + b_ := \integers..`+`(a_, b_)
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ plus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? + b_?"
. description: "Addition of integers."
Id: "d2bd8cc9-3dd0-4a49-8e85-cf5009edb9a4"


Text: "Multiplication of integers is associative and distributes over addition."
Id: "52cc558f-f07b-4b85-a932-7f4448a88b65"


[a_ \.integer.*./ b_]
Defines: a_ * b_ is \binary.operation:on{\integers}
expresses: a_ * b_ := \integers..`*`(a_, b_)
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ times $b_?$"
. written: "a_? \cdot b_?"
. description: "Multiplication of integers."
Id: "155efde4-346b-4674-8578-dfbffcdc188d"


Text: "Every integer $a$ has a unique additive inverse $-a$ satisfying
       $a + (-a) = 0$."
Id: "667c2087-7c50-4b19-b052-b9fa56d77656"


[\integer.neg(a_)]
Defines: n := \integers..neg(a_) is \integer
Documented:
. called: "negative of $a_?$"
. written: "-a_?"
. description: "The additive inverse (negation) of an integer $-a$."
Id: "c17b9934-2a9c-4ccc-8556-c76abd992020"


[a \:integer.leq:/ b]
Declares: a is \integer
when: b is \integer
satisfies: \integers..leq(a, b)
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than or equal to $b?$"
. written: "a? \leq b?"
. description: "The canonical total order on the integers."
Id: "26ed2caa-2e69-428c-8a1b-0e4c09a48dbc"


SectionTitle: "Realization by Differences of Naturals"
Id: "bd42839a-010c-43f1-b720-c50cd2e5eef6"


Text: "The pair construction now supplies every component of the abstract
       integer tuple.  Its carrier is the quotient of $\mathbb{N} \times
       \mathbb{N}$ by the relation above.  Addition adds like coordinates,
       multiplication expands $(a-b)(c-d)$, negation swaps the coordinates,
       and order compares the represented differences without using integer
       subtraction."
Id: "f23b6e0a-72bf-47a9-8ba0-6b48734992d1"


[\integer.pair.carrier]
Defines: Z := (\naturals \.set.cross./ \naturals) \.mod./ \integers.relation is \set
Documented:
. called: "integer pair quotient"
. written: "(\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}) / {\sim}_{\mathbb{Z}}"
. description: "The quotient carrier whose class $[(a,b)]$ represents the formal
                difference $a-b$."
Id: "84f19ae7-2d2d-4c34-93c7-a9c7386df968"


[\integer.pair.class:of{a, b}]
Defines: C := \equivalence.class:of{(a, b)}:over{\integers.relation}#using{X := \naturals \.set.cross./ \naturals} "in" \integer.pair.carrier
when: a, b is \natural
Documented:
. called: "integer class of $a?$ and $b?$"
. written: "[(a?, b?)]_{\mathbb{Z}}"
. description: "The equivalence class of the pair $(a,b)$ in the difference
                construction of the integers."
Id: "5c228ea8-d53f-4460-8909-56b46f19be2a"


[\integer.pair.zero]
Defines: z := \integer.pair.class:of{\natural.zero, \natural.zero} "in" \integer.pair.carrier
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair zero"
. written: "[(0,0)]_{\mathbb{Z}}"
Id: "c79011b3-a5f5-4225-b491-e9a4df1889b0"


[\integer.pair.one]
Defines: o := \integer.pair.class:of{\natural.one, \natural.zero} "in" \integer.pair.carrier
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair one"
. written: "[(1,0)]_{\mathbb{Z}}"
Id: "46a6c27b-692f-4fb6-af09-dd9f9787d9d3"


[\integer.pair.add]
Defines: add(a_, b_) is \binary.operation:on{\integer.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: a1, b1, a2, b2 is \natural
  then: add(\integer.pair.class:of{a1, b1}, \integer.pair.class:of{a2, b2}) = \integer.pair.class:of{a1 \.natural.+./ a2, b1 \.natural.+./ b2}
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair addition"
. written: "a_? +_{\mathbb{Z}} b_?"
. description: "Addition induced on equivalence classes by
                $[(a,b)] + [(c,d)] = [(a+c,b+d)]$."
Id: "720771d7-3535-4c2a-864a-868fb794656c"


[\integer.pair.mul]
Defines: mul(a_, b_) is \binary.operation:on{\integer.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: a1, b1, a2, b2 is \natural
  then:
  . let: ac, bd, ad, bc is \natural
    where:
    . ac = a1 \.natural.*./ a2
    . bd = b1 \.natural.*./ b2
    . ad = a1 \.natural.*./ b2
    . bc = b1 \.natural.*./ a2
    then: mul(\integer.pair.class:of{a1, b1}, \integer.pair.class:of{a2, b2}) = \integer.pair.class:of{ac \.natural.+./ bd, ad \.natural.+./ bc}
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair multiplication"
. written: "a_? \cdot_{\mathbb{Z}} b_?"
. description: "Multiplication induced by the identity
                $(a-b)(c-d)=(ac+bd)-(ad+bc)$."
Id: "851822e5-633e-477d-aaa9-175898ceb06e"


[\integer.pair.neg(a_)]
Defines: n(a_) is \function:on{\integer.pair.carrier}:to{\integer.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: p, q is \natural
  then: n(\integer.pair.class:of{p, q}) = \integer.pair.class:of{q, p}
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair negation"
. written: "-_{\mathbb{Z}} a_?"
. description: "Negation induced by swapping the coordinates:
                $-[(a,b)]=[(b,a)]$."
Id: "d3661a1d-1da3-47ef-9d54-cc841b4dcdda"


[\integer.pair.leq]
Defines: leq(a_, b_) is \total.order:on{\integer.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll: a1, b1, a2, b2 is \natural
  then:
  . let: lhs, rhs is \natural
    where:
    . lhs = a1 \.natural.+./ b2
    . rhs = a2 \.natural.+./ b1
    then:
    . have: leq(\integer.pair.class:of{a1, b1}, \integer.pair.class:of{a2, b2})
      iff: lhs \:natural.leq?:/ rhs
Documented:
. called: "integer-pair order"
. written: "a_? \leq_{\mathbb{Z}} b_?"
. description: "The quotient order $[(a,b)] \le [(c,d)]$ exactly when
                $a+d \le c+b$."
Id: "58e8d2d9-b0e3-416b-8536-3fb3a8b9063a"


[\integer.pair.realization]
Realizes: Zb ::= (Z, 0, 1, +, *, neg(z_), leq(a_, b_)) := \integers
specifies:
. Z := \integer.pair.carrier
. 0 := \integer.pair.zero
. 1 := \integer.pair.one
. + := \integer.pair.add
. * := \integer.pair.mul
. neg(z_) := \integer.pair.neg(z_)
. leq := \integer.pair.leq
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Zb :<->: x_ "in" Z
Documented:
. called: "integers as differences of naturals"
. description: "The concrete realization of the abstract integer tuple by
                equivalence classes of pairs of natural numbers."
Id: "b28cb699-77da-4c6f-92a3-3ffdec43ffcc"


SectionTitle: "Universal Property and Arithmetic"
Id: "ff363d3f-e5fd-4ddf-86e4-8d4182a49b2c"


Text: "The integer represented by a pair $(a,b)$ of natural numbers is the
       formal difference $a-b$. The structured realization above defines the
       equivalence relation, quotient carrier, and induced operations. Its
       well-definedness obligations ensure that changing representatives does
       not change a sum or product."
Id: "b495575c-dc6b-4665-bc56-e1c8c315eb8e"


TextTheorem: "The additive monoid $\mathbb{N}$ embeds into the additive group
              $\mathbb{Z}$ by $n\mapsto[n,0]$. More generally, every monoid
              homomorphism from $\mathbb{N}$ into an abelian group extends
              uniquely to a group homomorphism from $\mathbb{Z}$. This universal
              property characterizes the integers as the group completion of the
              natural numbers."
Id: "507603fd-5d69-4a75-8c86-692c2bc5ae35"


[a \:integer.less:/ b]
Declares: a is \integer
when: b is \integer
satisfies:
. a \:integer.leq:/ b
. a != b
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than $b?$"
. written: "a? < b?"
Id: "ad8694fc-bab4-469f-a127-2a6856e58faa"


Theorem:
given: a, b is \integer
where: \integer.zero \:integer.less?:/ b
then:
. existsUnique: q is \integer
  suchThat:
  . existsUnique: r is \integer
    suchThat:
    . a = (b \.integer.*./ q) \.integer.+./ r
    . \integer.zero \:integer.leq?:/ r
    . r \:integer.less?:/ b
Documented:
. called: "Euclidean Division"
. description: "For $b>0$, there are unique integers $q$ and $r$ such that
                $a=bq+r$ and $0\leq r<b$."
Id: "5dd9f7fc-9815-4816-bc2a-737babe16bf9"


[a \.integer.divides./ b]
States:
when: a, b is \integer
that:
. exists: k is \integer
  suchThat: b = a \.integer.*./ k
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ divides $b?$"
. written: "a? \mid b?"
Id: "95d6d9fd-e095-4ebf-a659-6ab4ecc44fd0"


[\greatest.common.divisor:of{a, b}]
Defines: d is \integer
when: a, b is \integer
expresses:
. if:
  . anyOf:
    . a != \integer.zero
    . b != \integer.zero
  then:
  . \integer.zero \:integer.less?:/ d
  . d \.integer.divides./ a
  . d \.integer.divides./ b
  . forAll: c is \integer
    where:
    . c \.integer.divides./ a
    . c \.integer.divides./ b
    then: c \.integer.divides./ d
. if:
  . a = \integer.zero
  . b = \integer.zero
  then: d = \integer.zero
. exists: x, y is \integer
  suchThat: d = (a \.integer.*./ x) \.integer.+./ (b \.integer.*./ y)
Documented:
. called: "greatest common divisor of $a?$ and $b?$"
. written: "\gcd(a?, b?)"
. description: "For a pair not both zero, the greatest common divisor is the
                unique positive common divisor divisible by every common
                divisor. Bezout's identity is included in the characterization;
                the definition is totalized by the convention $\gcd(0,0)=0$."
Id: "3f6acf7f-8ea8-4080-a9bc-23cddc72bdd5"
